<div class="slide-inner slide-cover"><span class="badge">Module 1 of 5</span>
<h2>Foundations of Population &amp; Demographic Statistics</h2><p class="msub">Core demographic concepts, population change, data sources and quality.</p>

<p class="lead">Demographic statistics describe the size, composition, distribution and change of human populations. They support planning for education, health, labour markets, housing, social protection and regional development across the East African Community.</p>
<h4 class="subh">In this module you will:</h4>
<div class="obj-grid">
<div class="obj"><div class="n">1</div><div>Define population, demography and demographic statistics.</div></div>
<div class="obj"><div class="n">2</div><div>Explain the demographic balancing equation.</div></div>
<div class="obj"><div class="n">3</div><div>Distinguish stocks, flows, rates, ratios and proportions.</div></div>
<div class="obj"><div class="n">4</div><div>Identify major demographic data sources.</div></div>
<div class="obj"><div class="n">5</div><div>Apply basic data-quality principles.</div></div>
</div>
<div class="outcomes"><h4>✅ By the end you will be able to:</h4><ul><li>Explain how births, deaths and migration change a population.</li><li>Select appropriate sources for common demographic indicators.</li><li>Recognise common quality problems such as undercoverage, age misstatement and delayed registration.</li></ul></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="1">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">2</div><h3>What is demography?</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p class="lead">Demography is the statistical study of human populations. It examines how many people there are, who they are, where they live and how populations change over time.</p>
<div class="cards">
<div class="bcard"><div class="p">Population size</div><div class="e">The number of people in a defined geographic area at a specified reference time.</div></div>
<div class="bcard"><div class="p">Population structure</div><div class="e">Composition by age, sex and other characteristics.</div></div>
<div class="bcard"><div class="p">Population distribution</div><div class="e">How people are spatially distributed across regions, districts, urban and rural areas.</div></div>
<div class="bcard"><div class="p">Population dynamics</div><div class="e">Change caused by fertility, mortality and migration.</div></div>
</div>
<div class="callout info"><h4>Why demographic statistics matter</h4><p>Population statistics provide denominators for many indicators. For example, health, education and labour statistics often require reliable population estimates by age and sex.</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="2">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">3</div><h3>The demographic balancing equation</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p>The population at the end of a period is determined by the opening population plus births and immigration, less deaths and emigration.</p>
<div class="formula">P₂ = P₁ + B − D + I − E<small>P₁ = opening population; B = births; D = deaths; I = immigrants; E = emigrants; P₂ = closing population</small></div>
<div class="example"><h4>🧮 Hypothetical EAC example</h4><p>A district begins the year with 800,000 people. During the year there are 24,000 births, 6,000 deaths, 9,000 immigrants and 5,000 emigrants.</p><div class="formula">800,000 + 24,000 − 6,000 + 9,000 − 5,000 = 822,000</div><div class="key">The district ends the year with an estimated population of 822,000.</div></div>
<div class="quiz-inline" data-answer="2" data-explain="Population change is driven directly by births, deaths, immigration and emigration.">
<div class="q-title">Knowledge check: Which set contains the direct components of population change?</div>
<button class="opt">Economic growth, inflation and employment</button>
<button class="opt">School enrolment, households and rainfall</button>
<button class="opt">Births, deaths, immigration and emigration</button>
<button class="opt">Exports, imports, taxes and grants</button>
<div class="q-feedback"></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="3">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">4</div><h3>Stocks, flows, ratios and rates</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td class="rh">Stock</td><td>A quantity measured at a point in time.</td><td>Population on census night.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Flow</td><td>Events occurring during a period.</td><td>Births registered during a year.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Proportion</td><td>A part divided by the whole; numerator is included in denominator.</td><td>Share of population aged 0–14.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Ratio</td><td>One quantity divided by another distinct quantity.</td><td>Sex ratio: males per 100 females.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Rate</td><td>Frequency of events relative to population exposed to the event, usually over time.</td><td>Crude birth rate per 1,000 population.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="callout tip"><h4>Correct denominator matters</h4><p>A demographic rate should use the population actually exposed to the event whenever possible. Age-specific rates therefore provide more analytical detail than crude rates.</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="4">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">5</div><h3>Major demographic data sources</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="cards">
<div class="bcard"><div class="p">🏠 Population & housing census</div><div class="e">Near-complete enumeration at a specified reference time; provides small-area population structure and distribution.</div></div>
<div class="bcard"><div class="p">🧾 CRVS</div><div class="e">Civil registration and vital statistics record births, deaths and other vital events continuously.</div></div>
<div class="bcard"><div class="p">📋 Household surveys</div><div class="e">Sample-based sources that can collect fertility, mortality, migration and socioeconomic characteristics.</div></div>
<div class="bcard"><div class="p">🏛️ Administrative systems</div><div class="e">School, health, immigration, social security and other operational records can complement demographic sources.</div></div>
<div class="bcard"><div class="p">🪪 Population registers</div><div class="e">Continuous systems that maintain information on resident populations where such registers exist.</div></div>
</div>
<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Often suitable source</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td class="rh">Small-area population count</td><td>Census</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Continuous births and deaths</td><td>CRVS</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Detailed fertility history</td><td>Household survey</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Border movements</td><td>Immigration/administrative records, complemented by surveys or census</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="5">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">6</div><h3>Data quality and metadata</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p>High-quality demographic statistics require transparent concepts, consistent classifications and well-documented methods.</p>
<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Quality dimension</th><th>Questions to ask</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td class="rh">Coverage</td><td>Were all intended people or events captured? Is there undercount or duplication?</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Accuracy</td><td>Are ages, dates, sex and locations correctly reported and coded?</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Timeliness</td><td>How quickly are results available after the reference period?</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Coherence</td><td>Are census, survey, CRVS and administrative estimates reasonably consistent?</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Comparability</td><td>Are concepts and methods stable across places and time?</td></tr>
<tr><td class="rh">Accessibility</td><td>Can users find data, metadata and explanations?</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="callout warn"><h4>Common demographic data issues</h4><p>Age heaping, under-registration of births or deaths, household omissions, double counting, misclassification of residence and inconsistent geographic boundaries can distort indicators.</p></div>
<div class="quiz-inline" data-answer="1" data-explain="A census is designed to provide population counts and characteristics for small geographic areas at a specific reference time.">
<div class="q-title">Knowledge check: Which source is usually strongest for detailed small-area population counts?</div>
<button class="opt">A small sample survey</button><button class="opt">Population and housing census</button><button class="opt">Price index</button><button class="opt">Trade statistics</button><div class="q-feedback"></div></div>
</div></div></div><div class="deck" data-mid="m2" data-n="6" id="deck-m2"><div class="slide active" data-i="0">
<div class="slide-inner slide-cover"><span class="badge">Module 2 of 5</span>
<h2>Population Size, Structure &amp; Distribution</h2><p class="msub">Age-sex composition, dependency, density and population growth.</p>

<p class="lead">Population structure turns a total headcount into an analytical profile. Age, sex and location reveal demand for schools, jobs, health services, housing and social protection.</p>
<div class="obj-grid"><div class="obj"><div class="n">1</div><div>Measure population composition by age and sex.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">2</div><div>Calculate sex ratio and dependency ratio.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">3</div><div>Interpret population pyramids.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">4</div><div>Calculate population density and growth.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">5</div><div>Explain the demographic dividend concept.</div></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="1">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">2</div><h3>Age and sex structure</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p>Age and sex are two of the most important demographic variables. Standard age groups often include single years, five-year groups, or broad functional groups such as 0–14, 15–64 and 65+.</p>
<div class="formula">Sex Ratio = (Number of males ÷ Number of females) × 100<small>Often expressed as males per 100 females.</small></div>
<div class="example"><h4>🧮 Example</h4><p>If a population has 510,000 males and 500,000 females:</p><div class="formula">(510,000 ÷ 500,000) × 100 = 102</div><div class="key">There are 102 males for every 100 females.</div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="2">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">3</div><h3>Dependency ratios</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p>A dependency ratio compares populations commonly considered economically dependent with the working-age population. It is a structural indicator, not a direct measure of actual employment or dependency.</p>
<div class="formula">Total Dependency Ratio = ((P₀₋₁₄ + P₆₅₊) ÷ P₁₅₋₆₄) × 100</div>
<div class="cards"><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Child dependency ratio</div><div class="e">Population aged 0–14 relative to working-age population.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Old-age dependency ratio</div><div class="e">Population aged 65+ relative to working-age population.</div></div></div>
<div class="example"><h4>🧮 Example</h4><p>A population has 420,000 people aged 0–14, 530,000 aged 15–64 and 50,000 aged 65+.</p><div class="formula">((420,000 + 50,000) ÷ 530,000) × 100 ≈ 88.7</div><div class="key">There are about 89 people in the dependent-age groups per 100 people aged 15–64.</div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="3">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">4</div><h3>Population pyramids</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p>A population pyramid plots age groups vertically and population counts or percentages horizontally, typically showing males and females on opposite sides.</p>
<div class="cards"><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Expansive shape</div><div class="e">Broad base and narrower upper ages; often associated with relatively high fertility and a young population.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">More stationary shape</div><div class="e">Similar widths across many age groups; suggests slower change in age structure.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Constrictive shape</div><div class="e">Narrower younger cohorts relative to middle ages; often associated with lower fertility and population ageing.</div></div></div>
<div class="callout info"><h4>Look for irregularities</h4><p>Bulges, indentations and sex imbalances may reflect past fertility changes, mortality shocks, migration, conflict, boundary changes or data-quality problems.</p></div>
<div class="quiz-inline" data-answer="0" data-explain="A broad base indicates large younger cohorts, commonly associated with relatively high fertility."><div class="q-title">Knowledge check: What does a very broad base on a population pyramid usually suggest?</div><button class="opt">Large younger cohorts and relatively high fertility</button><button class="opt">No migration at all</button><button class="opt">Zero mortality</button><button class="opt">Equal population at every age</button><div class="q-feedback"></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="4">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">5</div><h3>Population density and distribution</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="formula">Population Density = Population ÷ Land Area (km²)</div>
<p>Density is useful for comparing settlement concentration, but national or regional averages can hide major local differences.</p>
<div class="example"><h4>🧮 Hypothetical example</h4><p>A region has 2,400,000 residents and a land area of 30,000 km².</p><div class="formula">2,400,000 ÷ 30,000 = 80 persons/km²</div></div>
<div class="callout tip"><h4>Distribution is more than density</h4><p>Maps, urban-rural classifications and subnational tabulations help identify where people are concentrated and how access to services varies spatially.</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="5">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">6</div><h3>Population growth and the demographic dividend</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="formula">Growth Rate (%) ≈ ((P₂ − P₁) ÷ P₁) × 100<small>For a simple one-period calculation. Compound annual growth is preferable across multiple years.</small></div>
<p>The <strong>demographic dividend</strong> refers to potential economic benefits when the working-age share of the population rises relative to dependent ages. It is not automatic: outcomes depend on health, education, skills, employment, productivity and institutions.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><h4>Interpret carefully</h4><p>A lower dependency ratio does not itself guarantee higher economic growth. It creates a potential opportunity that requires supportive policies and labour-market absorption.</p></div>
<div class="quiz-inline" data-answer="3" data-explain="The demographic dividend is a potential benefit associated with age-structure change, but it depends on policies and economic conditions."><div class="q-title">Knowledge check: Which statement about the demographic dividend is correct?</div><button class="opt">It is guaranteed whenever fertility falls.</button><button class="opt">It means everyone aged 15–64 is employed.</button><button class="opt">It measures only migration.</button><button class="opt">It is a potential benefit that depends on supportive investments and jobs.</button><div class="q-feedback"></div></div>
</div></div></div><div class="deck" data-mid="m3" data-n="7" id="deck-m3"><div class="slide active" data-i="0">
<div class="slide-inner slide-cover"><span class="badge">Module 3 of 5</span>
<h2>Fertility &amp; Reproduction</h2><p class="msub">Birth measures, age-specific fertility, TFR and reproductive indicators.</p>

<p class="lead">Fertility statistics measure the actual occurrence of live births in a population. They are central to understanding population growth and future age structure.</p>
<div class="obj-grid"><div class="obj"><div class="n">1</div><div>Distinguish fertility from fecundity.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">2</div><div>Calculate crude and general fertility rates.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">3</div><div>Interpret age-specific fertility rates.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">4</div><div>Explain total fertility rate.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">5</div><div>Identify fertility data sources and quality issues.</div></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="1">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">2</div><h3>Fertility concepts</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="def"><div class="t">Fertility</div><div class="x">Actual childbearing performance, commonly measured through live births.</div></div>
<div class="def"><div class="t">Fecundity</div><div class="x">Biological capacity to reproduce. Fecundity and observed fertility are not the same.</div></div>
<p>Fertility is influenced by demographic, social, economic, cultural and behavioural factors, including age at union, contraceptive use, breastfeeding, education and desired family size.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="2">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">3</div><h3>Crude birth rate and general fertility rate</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="formula">CBR = (Live Births ÷ Mid-year Population) × 1,000</div>
<div class="formula">GFR = (Live Births ÷ Women aged 15–49) × 1,000</div>
<div class="example"><h4>🧮 Example</h4><p>A population of 1,000,000 records 28,000 live births. There are 250,000 women aged 15–49.</p><ul><li>CBR = 28,000 ÷ 1,000,000 × 1,000 = <strong>28 per 1,000 population</strong>.</li><li>GFR = 28,000 ÷ 250,000 × 1,000 = <strong>112 per 1,000 women aged 15–49</strong>.</li></ul></div>
<div class="callout info"><h4>Why “crude”?</h4><p>The crude birth rate uses the total population in its denominator, including people not exposed to childbearing. It is easy to compute but strongly affected by age-sex structure.</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="3">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">4</div><h3>Age-specific fertility rates (ASFR)</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="formula">ASFRₓ = (Births to women in age group x ÷ Women in age group x) × 1,000</div>
<p>ASFRs show the age pattern of childbearing and allow comparisons that are less distorted by differences in age structure.</p>
<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Age group</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="rh">15–19</td><td>Adolescent fertility.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">20–24 to 30–34</td><td>Often contain the largest concentration of births, depending on the population.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">35–49</td><td>Later-age fertility.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="quiz-inline" data-answer="2" data-explain="ASFR uses women in a specific age group as the population exposed to births in that age group."><div class="q-title">Knowledge check: Which denominator is used for an ASFR for women aged 20–24?</div><button class="opt">Total national population</button><button class="opt">All women aged 15–49</button><button class="opt">Women aged 20–24</button><button class="opt">All households</button><div class="q-feedback"></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="4">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">5</div><h3>Total fertility rate (TFR)</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p>The total fertility rate summarises age-specific fertility rates into the average number of children a woman would have if she experienced the current age-specific fertility schedule throughout her reproductive years, subject to the assumptions of the period measure.</p>
<div class="formula">TFR = 5 × ΣASFR ÷ 1,000<small>When ASFRs are expressed per 1,000 women in five-year age groups.</small></div>
<div class="callout tip"><h4>TFR is a synthetic cohort measure</h4><p>It describes the fertility schedule of a particular period. It is not the observed completed family size of an actual group of women unless the schedule remains unchanged.</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="5">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">6</div><h3>Other reproductive indicators</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="cards"><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Gross reproduction rate</div><div class="e">Average number of daughters a woman would bear under current fertility rates, ignoring female mortality.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Net reproduction rate</div><div class="e">Average number of daughters a newborn girl would bear over her lifetime under current fertility and female mortality schedules.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Mean age at childbearing</div><div class="e">Average age of mothers at childbirth, weighted by age-specific fertility.</div></div></div>
<div class="callout info"><h4>Replacement-level fertility</h4><p>Replacement level refers to the fertility required for one generation to replace itself in the long run in the absence of migration. The exact level depends on mortality conditions and the sex ratio at birth; it should not be treated as a universal fixed constant for every population.</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="6">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">7</div><h3>Fertility data sources and quality</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Strength</th><th>Typical limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="rh">CRVS</td><td>Continuous registration of births where complete.</td><td>Under-registration or delayed registration may bias rates.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">Census</td><td>Large coverage; can collect recent births and children ever born.</td><td>Recall and reporting errors; limited detail.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">Household survey</td><td>Detailed birth histories and covariates.</td><td>Sampling error and retrospective recall error.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="quiz-inline" data-answer="1" data-explain="TFR is constructed from age-specific fertility rates and summarizes the current age pattern of fertility."><div class="q-title">Knowledge check: Which measure is built directly from age-specific fertility rates?</div><button class="opt">Population density</button><button class="opt">Total fertility rate</button><button class="opt">Sex ratio</button><button class="opt">Net migration rate</button><div class="q-feedback"></div></div>
</div></div></div><div class="deck" data-mid="m4" data-n="7" id="deck-m4"><div class="slide active" data-i="0">
<div class="slide-inner slide-cover"><span class="badge">Module 4 of 5</span>
<h2>Mortality &amp; Survival</h2><p class="msub">Death rates, infant and child mortality, life tables and life expectancy.</p>

<p class="lead">Mortality statistics measure the occurrence of deaths and survival in populations. They are fundamental for health planning, actuarial analysis and population projections.</p>
<div class="obj-grid"><div class="obj"><div class="n">1</div><div>Calculate crude and age-specific death rates.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">2</div><div>Interpret infant and under-five mortality measures.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">3</div><div>Explain direct and indirect standardization.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">4</div><div>Understand life-table functions.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">5</div><div>Explain life expectancy.</div></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="1">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">2</div><h3>Crude and specific death rates</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="formula">CDR = (Deaths during year ÷ Mid-year Population) × 1,000</div>
<div class="formula">Age-specific Death Rate = (Deaths at age x ÷ Population at age x) × k</div>
<div class="example"><h4>🧮 Example</h4><p>If 7,500 deaths occur in a mid-year population of 1,250,000:</p><div class="formula">(7,500 ÷ 1,250,000) × 1,000 = 6.0 deaths per 1,000 population</div></div>
<div class="callout warn"><h4>Crude rates can mislead comparisons</h4><p>Two populations can have different crude death rates simply because one population is older. Age-standardized rates are often preferable for comparing mortality levels.</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="2">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">3</div><h3>Infant and child mortality</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="formula">IMR = (Deaths under age 1 ÷ Live births) × 1,000</div>
<div class="cards"><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Neonatal mortality</div><div class="e">Deaths in the first 28 completed days of life relative to live births.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Post-neonatal mortality</div><div class="e">Deaths after the neonatal period but before age one.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Under-five mortality</div><div class="e">Probability of dying before age five, usually expressed per 1,000 live births.</div></div></div>
<div class="callout info"><h4>Rate vs probability</h4><p>Some child mortality indicators are formally probabilities derived from life-table or survey methods, even though they are often informally called “rates.”</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="3">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">4</div><h3>Mortality standardization</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p>Standardization removes the effect of differing age structures so mortality levels can be compared more fairly.</p>
<div class="cards"><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Direct standardization</div><div class="e">Applies observed age-specific rates to a common standard population.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Indirect standardization</div><div class="e">Applies standard age-specific rates to the study population; often summarized using a standardized mortality ratio.</div></div></div>
<div class="quiz-inline" data-answer="0" data-explain="Age standardization helps separate true mortality differences from differences caused by age composition."><div class="q-title">Knowledge check: Why is age standardization useful when comparing mortality?</div><button class="opt">It reduces distortion from different age structures.</button><button class="opt">It guarantees complete death registration.</button><button class="opt">It converts deaths into births.</button><button class="opt">It measures migration.</button><div class="q-feedback"></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="4">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">5</div><h3>Life tables</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p>A life table describes the mortality experience of a hypothetical cohort subjected to a set of age-specific mortality rates.</p>
<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="rh">lₓ</td><td>Number of survivors to exact age x from the life-table radix.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">dₓ</td><td>Deaths between exact ages x and x+n.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">qₓ</td><td>Probability of dying between x and x+n.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">Lₓ</td><td>Person-years lived between x and x+n.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">Tₓ</td><td>Total person-years remaining after age x.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">eₓ</td><td>Expectation of life at age x.</td></tr></tbody></table>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="5">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">6</div><h3>Life expectancy</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="def"><div class="t">Life expectancy at birth</div><div class="x">The average number of years a newborn would live if the current age-specific mortality rates remained in effect throughout life.</div></div>
<p>Life expectancy is a period summary of current mortality conditions. It is not a prediction of exactly how long any individual newborn will live.</p>
<div class="formula">eₓ = Tₓ ÷ lₓ<small>Life-table relationship between remaining person-years and survivors at exact age x.</small></div>
<div class="quiz-inline" data-answer="2" data-explain="Life expectancy summarizes the mortality schedule; it is not an individual prediction."><div class="q-title">Knowledge check: What does life expectancy at birth summarize?</div><button class="opt">Future GDP growth</button><button class="opt">Total migration over a decade</button><button class="opt">Current age-specific mortality conditions</button><button class="opt">Only infant deaths</button><div class="q-feedback"></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="6">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">7</div><h3>Mortality data sources and quality</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="cards"><div class="bcard"><div class="p">CRVS</div><div class="e">Preferred continuous source for deaths and causes of death where registration is complete and medically certified.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Census and surveys</div><div class="e">Can estimate adult and child mortality where CRVS is incomplete, using household deaths, birth histories or survival questions.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Health systems</div><div class="e">Facility records support cause-specific analysis but may not represent all deaths in the population.</div></div></div>
<div class="callout warn"><h4>Completeness is critical</h4><p>If deaths are under-registered, crude and age-specific mortality rates can be biased downward. Analysts should assess completeness before interpretation.</p></div>
</div></div></div><div class="deck" data-mid="m5" data-n="8" id="deck-m5"><div class="slide active" data-i="0">
<div class="slide-inner slide-cover"><span class="badge">Module 5 of 5</span>
<h2>Migration, Urbanization &amp; Population Projections</h2><p class="msub">Migration measures, urban change and cohort-component projections.</p>

<p class="lead">Migration can rapidly reshape local populations, labour markets and service demand. Population projections then combine fertility, mortality and migration assumptions to estimate future population size and structure.</p>
<div class="obj-grid"><div class="obj"><div class="n">1</div><div>Define internal and international migration.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">2</div><div>Calculate net migration and migration rates.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">3</div><div>Explain urbanization measures.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">4</div><div>Distinguish estimates from projections.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">5</div><div>Explain the cohort-component method.</div></div><div class="obj"><div class="n">6</div><div>Interpret projection scenarios and uncertainty.</div></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="1">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">2</div><h3>Migration concepts</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="def"><div class="t">Migration</div><div class="x">A change in usual residence from one geographic area to another, defined using a specified spatial boundary and time criterion.</div></div>
<div class="cards"><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Internal migration</div><div class="e">Movement between areas within the same country.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">International migration</div><div class="e">Movement involving a change of country of usual residence.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">In-migration / immigration</div><div class="e">Movement into the area being studied.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Out-migration / emigration</div><div class="e">Movement out of the area being studied.</div></div></div>
<p>Definitions of “usual residence” and duration should be documented because they affect who is classified as a migrant.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="2">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">3</div><h3>Migration measures</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="formula">Net Migration = In-migrants − Out-migrants</div>
<div class="formula">Net Migration Rate = (Net Migration ÷ Mid-year Population) × 1,000</div>
<div class="example"><h4>🧮 Example</h4><p>A region records 18,000 in-migrants and 11,000 out-migrants in a population of 700,000.</p><ul><li>Net migration = 18,000 − 11,000 = <strong>7,000</strong>.</li><li>Net migration rate = 7,000 ÷ 700,000 × 1,000 = <strong>10 per 1,000</strong>.</li></ul></div>
<div class="quiz-inline" data-answer="1" data-explain="Net migration is inflows minus outflows."><div class="q-title">Knowledge check: If immigration is 30,000 and emigration is 38,000, net migration equals:</div><button class="opt">+68,000</button><button class="opt">−8,000</button><button class="opt">+8,000</button><button class="opt">0</button><div class="q-feedback"></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="3">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">4</div><h3>Urbanization</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="def"><div class="t">Urbanization</div><div class="x">Increase over time in the proportion of a population living in areas classified as urban.</div></div>
<div class="formula">Urban Share (%) = (Urban Population ÷ Total Population) × 100</div>
<p>Urban population growth can result from natural increase within urban areas, rural-to-urban migration, international migration, and reclassification or expansion of urban boundaries.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><h4>Comparability issue</h4><p>Urban definitions differ across countries and can change over time. Regional comparisons should always review national definitions and boundary changes.</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="4">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">5</div><h3>Population estimates vs projections</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="rh">Population estimate</td><td>An assessment of population size or structure for a past or current date, using available evidence.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">Population projection</td><td>A conditional calculation of future population based on specified assumptions about fertility, mortality and migration.</td></tr><tr><td class="rh">Forecast</td><td>A projection presented as the analyst's judgement of a likely future path.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="callout info"><h4>Projections are conditional</h4><p>A projection is not a certainty. Its results depend on the base population, model and assumptions.</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="5">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">6</div><h3>The cohort-component method</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p>The cohort-component method advances each age-sex cohort over time, applying mortality, adding births and incorporating migration.</p>
<div class="cards"><div class="bcard"><div class="p">1 · Base population</div><div class="e">Start with a population by age and sex for a defined date.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">2 · Survival</div><div class="e">Apply survival probabilities to move cohorts into older ages.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">3 · Births</div><div class="e">Apply fertility assumptions to women of reproductive age and distribute births by sex.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">4 · Migration</div><div class="e">Add or subtract migrants by age and sex.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">5 · Repeat</div><div class="e">Continue for each projection interval.</div></div></div>
<div class="formula">Future population = Surviving population + Births + Net migration<small>Applied by age and sex in a cohort-component framework.</small></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="6">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">7</div><h3>Projection scenarios and uncertainty</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<p>Projection exercises commonly use multiple scenarios to show how alternative assumptions affect results.</p>
<div class="cards"><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Low scenario</div><div class="e">A defined combination of assumptions producing relatively lower population growth.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">Medium scenario</div><div class="e">A central or reference set of assumptions.</div></div><div class="bcard"><div class="p">High scenario</div><div class="e">A defined combination producing relatively higher growth.</div></div></div>
<div class="callout tip"><h4>Uncertainty grows with horizon</h4><p>Near-term projections are strongly influenced by the existing age structure. Longer-term results become increasingly sensitive to fertility, mortality and migration assumptions.</p></div>
<div class="quiz-inline" data-answer="3" data-explain="The cohort-component method explicitly projects age-sex cohorts using fertility, mortality and migration."><div class="q-title">Knowledge check: Which method explicitly applies fertility, mortality and migration to age-sex cohorts?</div><button class="opt">Consumer price index</button><button class="opt">Input-output table</button><button class="opt">Simple trade balance</button><button class="opt">Cohort-component method</button><div class="q-feedback"></div></div>
</div></div>
<div class="slide " data-i="7">
<div class="slide-inner"><div class="slide-head"><div class="sn">8</div><h3>Summary &amp; key takeaways</h3><span class="st">Learning slide</span></div>

<div class="takeaways"><h4>🎯 Course-level takeaways</h4>
<div class="tk"><div class="n">1</div><div>Population change is determined by births, deaths, immigration and emigration.</div></div>
<div class="tk"><div class="n">2</div><div>Age-sex structure affects crude demographic rates and determines many planning needs.</div></div>
<div class="tk"><div class="n">3</div><div>Fertility is best understood through age-specific measures and summary indicators such as TFR.</div></div>
<div class="tk"><div class="n">4</div><div>Mortality analysis uses age-specific rates, child mortality measures and life tables; comparisons often require standardization.</div></div>
<div class="tk"><div class="n">5</div><div>Migration and urbanization can reshape populations quickly, while projections show conditional future paths.</div></div>
<div class="tk"><div class="n">6</div><div>Good demographic analysis depends on strong census, CRVS, survey and administrative data, supported by clear metadata and quality assessment.</div></div>
</div>
<div class="teaser"><h3>🎓 Ready for the final assessment?</h3><p>The final assessment draws questions from all five modules. A score of 75% or higher earns the course certificate.</p></div>
</div></div></div></div>
